# Phase Commits

Work is committed one phase at a time. A phase is committed only once it is genuinely
finished, so the branch history reads as a sequence of complete, working steps.

## When to commit a phase

A phase is ready to commit when both of these are true:

- The tests for that phase pass. Run `npm test` and confirm it is green.
- The technical PRD in `ai-workspace/` has been updated with the phase status and details.

Until both hold, the phase is not finished. Do not commit partial work in the middle of a
phase, and never commit while its tests are failing.

## Making the commit

- Stage only the files belonging to that phase. Use explicit paths with `git add <path>`
  rather than `git add -A` or `git add .`, which pick up unrelated work.
- Commit with the phase number and a short description of what the phase delivered:

```bash
git commit -m "phase 3: add MCQ list and delete endpoints"
```

- Push to the remote branch immediately after committing so the branch on the remote
  stays in step with local work.
- One phase per commit. Do not combine two phases into a single commit, and do not split
  one phase across several commits.

## What belongs in the commit

- PRD updates and D1 migrations that describe or support the phase go in the **same**
  commit as the code they relate to. Do not commit them separately.
- Anything not part of the phase stays out, including unrelated formatting or refactors.

## Fixes after a phase is closed

Once a phase is committed, that commit is closed. A later correction gets its own commit
prefixed `fix:`, with the related phase named in the commit body:

```bash
git commit -m "fix: reject blank MCQ option text" -m "Follow-up to phase 3."
```

Do not amend or rewrite a phase commit that has already been pushed.
